import{_ as t,o as i,c as a,a3 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Some Simpler Adaptive Setups","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"较简单的自适应装置们","archivedAt":"2026-10-27","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=kXUgC0EOTqKh09bVHL9EPN","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/some-simpler-adaptive-setups"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"some-simpler-adaptive-setups.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-kXUgC0EOTqKh09bVHL9EPN.md"}'),o={name:"some-simpler-adaptive-setups.md"};function r(s,e,l,u,d,p){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="some-simpler-adaptive-setups" tabindex="-1">Some Simpler Adaptive Setups <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-simpler-adaptive-setups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some Simpler Adaptive Setups&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>kXUgC0EOTqKh09bVHL9EPN</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=kXUgC0EOTqKh09bVHL9EPN" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p><p><strong>Translation note:</strong> This is a translation of a community draft, including its unfinished sections and comments. Here, <em>feedforward</em> (前馈) means controlling activation from the input side; <em>output-driven control</em> (后馈) means controlling it from the output side. Separately, <em>feedback control</em> (反馈控制) and <em>ratio control</em> (比例控制) describe how that control is implemented. These labels follow the author&#39;s usage. “Multi-unit” translates 多核 and refers to a group of production machines, not Protocol Automation-Cores. Source inconsistencies are flagged in the translator&#39;s notes at the end. Original image references are retained; text inside those images has not been translated.</p></blockquote><p>Coo coo coo. Coo coo. Coo coo coo coo coo. [Still procrastinating…]</p><p>言 is working on this…</p><p>This article introduces the adaptive setups we designed and mainly used in version 1.4, starting with the basics and gradually moving on to more complex designs. <span class="mark-red">Please read the setups in order.</span></p><p>I&#39;m so familiar with these designs that I may take things for granted when explaining them. If anything is unclear, please leave a comment.</p><p><s>kokobird: I&#39;m scared enough to cry…</s> Don&#39;t scare the readers away, boss! TAT</p><p>斯改: Please keep updating this. It&#39;s my only way of learning about advanced adaptive setups. (言: What&#39;s so advanced about them…)</p><p>kokobird: Boss, I just remembered that the video I posted for you already includes explanations of quite a few of these setups. See where you think it would fit: BV1CX3J6ZEhc</p><p>JNK: Keep in mind that feedforward and output-driven control are ways to prevent waste, allowing the reactions to be accounted for as standard recipes.</p><p>kokobird: Boss, I&#39;ve finished this section. It explains where the activation-material problem came from, how the solutions developed, and the concepts of feedforward and output-driven control. <a href="/endfield-skydoc/3-3-you-you-xiranite-freeloading-pig">3.3 You… You Xiranite-Guzzling Pig!</a></p><p><span id="source-block-pn4Iehl7uCFHMbdr1hj0r1"></span></p><h2 id="xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Xiragen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Xiragen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Production recipe: 1 Liquid Xiranite (activation) + 5 Liquid Xiranite = 5 Xiragen</p><p>P.S. Although the machine UI lists a “minimum rate of 6 units/min” for the activation material, each unit actually keeps the machine running for 10 s. There are exactly three situations in which activation material is wasted: ① the machine is active but lacks inputs; ② the machine is active but its output is blocked; ③ two units of activation material arrive less than 10 s apart.</p><p>Feedforward: the input supply rate determines the machine&#39;s operating rate.</p><p>Feedback control: the machine runs at a high rate when the input material overflows, and at a low rate when it does not.</p><p>P.S. Don&#39;t worry if feedforward, output-driven control, ratio control, and feedback control don&#39;t make sense yet. Keep reading and use the actual machines to build an understanding of these categories.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-a55f5a44821fbabd.q90-6cd1ff8531b46ce8.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>The priority created by the “Conduit Outlet Manifold–Pipe Converger” arrangement shown above (<a href="/endfield-skydoc/input-output-merge-priority-theory">Input / Output / Merge Priority Theory: A Brief Explanation</a>) sends Liquid Xiranite into the machine&#39;s material input first. Only after the machine fills up does Liquid Xiranite start entering the activation port. <u>In other words, whenever activation material enters, sufficient input material is already available.</u> Provided that the output remains unblocked—all the Xiragen produced is consumed downstream—and the activation supply does not exceed 6/min, no activation material will be wasted. A Pipe Control Port set to 6/min provides the required limit. [TN1]</p><p>When the input material—Liquid Xiranite—overflows, some of it reaches the activation port and the machine runs at its high rate of 30/min. If the incoming Liquid Xiranite supply is below 36/min, the machine consumes the accumulated material until it no longer overflows into the Conduit Outlet Manifold. Once that overflow stops, Liquid Xiranite no longer reaches the activation port, so the machine runs at its low rate of 0/min. If the incoming supply is above 0/min, material then accumulates until it overflows into the manifold again. This cycle repeats until, <u>over a sufficiently long period, the amount of Liquid Xiranite buffered in the setup neither increases nor decreases</u>. Here, “buffer” simply means the Liquid Xiranite retained within the setup. <u>This is what we mean by “feedback control.”</u> <span class="mark-grey">Or, for an input supply of 0/min–36/min: input overflows → the machine speeds up → input stops overflowing → the machine slows down → input overflows again, and so on.</span> [TN2]</p><p>Because the buffered Liquid Xiranite neither increases nor decreases over a sufficiently long period, the rate entering the setup equals the rate consumed by the machine. Viewed from the input side, the supply rate therefore controls the machine&#39;s operating rate. Looking at the actual sequence, every 1 Liquid Xiranite entering the activation port allows the machine to consume 5 Liquid Xiranite, since sufficient material is always available. Another 5 Liquid Xiranite must then enter the material input before another unit can reach the activation port. If the incoming supply rate is v1, the machine&#39;s actual reaction rate—its Xiragen output rate—is:</p><p><code>v1/(5+1)*5</code></p><p>Reading the numbers from left to right: 5 Liquid Xiranite enter the material input, 1 enters the activation port, and 5 Xiragen are produced. Once again, the input supply rate controls the machine&#39;s operating rate. <u>This is what we mean by “feedforward.”</u></p><p>At this point, the last two paragraphs may feel like a long way of stating the obvious. That&#39;s because this setup is simple enough to make the explanation seem redundant. If feedforward, feedback control, or even output-driven control becomes confusing later, come back to this simple case and use it to work through the more complicated ones.</p><p><span class="mark-grey"><em>The Conduit Outlet Manifold in the image is also labelled “Avoid connecting to a Conduit Inlet Manifold.” Connecting a manifold inlet to a manifold outlet allows liquid or gas transfer at 2–4/s. However, the “Conduit Outlet Manifold–Pipe Converger” priority arrangement sends any flow above 2/s into the lower-priority pipe, so its priority is not absolute. For example, suppose the instantaneous flow is “1/s for 1 s, 2/s for 1 s, then 3/s for 1 s.” Its average is 2/s, so you would expect nothing to enter the lower-priority pipe. In fact, during the second at 3/s, the priority pipe takes 2/s and the lower-priority pipe takes 1/s. The lower-priority pipe unexpectedly receives an average of 1/3/s! Connecting a single-port conduit endpoint to a manifold instead caps the flow at 2/s, giving the arrangement strict priority.</em></span></p><p><span class="mark-grey"><em>For now, call this the input-phase problem of manifold–Converger priority. Later sections call it the phase problem.</em></span></p><p><span id="source-block-jbMtAzUgYuD80KP2VIIVHK"></span></p><h2 id="xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Xiragen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Xiragen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Production recipe: 1 Liquid Xiranite (activation) + 5 Liquid Xiranite = 5 Xiragen</p><p>Feedforward: the input supply rate determines the machine&#39;s operating rate.</p><p>Ratio control: directly maintain a 5:1 ratio between input material and activation material.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-0cbc497c2d640327.q90-f746202a544dbbbb.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-7c3868649842b066.q90-39a7aee217dabc0e.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p><span class="mark-grey"><em>When using this setup, cap the incoming supply at 36/min—the actual flow may be below 36/min—or cap the Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit&#39;s activation input at 6/min.</em></span></p><p>The splitting arrangement shown above sends five times as much Liquid Xiranite into the material input as into the activation port. Because the recipe uses the same material for both purposes, <u>this is the simplest case of ratio control.</u> The activation flow is held at a fixed ratio to the material input flow, so the latter controls the former. Naturally, the input supply rate then controls the product output rate, or the machine&#39;s operating rate. <u>That is “feedforward.”</u></p><p><span id="source-block-rt7NWQgIE6noUMh21BwgbG"></span></p><h2 id="cuprium-gas-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Cuprium Gas: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#cuprium-gas-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cuprium Gas: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Production recipe: 1 Liquid Xiranite (activation) + 10 Cuprium Solution = 5 Cuprium Gas</p><p>Feedforward: the input supply rate determines the machine&#39;s operating rate.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-a495052c431e36ec.q90-2594755ba20cb8f0.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>Because of the priority created by the “Pipe Converger–Pipe Splitter” arrangement shown above (<a href="/endfield-skydoc/input-output-merge-priority-theory">Input / Output / Merge Priority Theory: A Brief Explanation</a>), <u>when the incoming </u><span class="mark-red"><u>Cuprium Solution</u></span><u> supply is between 30/min and 60/min,</u> <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> fills the machine before Liquid Xiranite increases its flow into the activation port from 3/min to 6/min. This means sufficient material is available whenever activation material enters. Provided that the output remains unblocked—all the <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Gas</span> produced is consumed downstream—and the activation supply remains below 6/min, no activation material will be wasted. A Pipe Control Port set to 6/min provides the limit. [TN1]</p><p><u>Why does an incoming </u><span class="mark-red"><u>Cuprium Solution</u></span><u> supply of 30/min–60/min ensure sufficient material whenever activation material arrives?</u> When <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> fills the machine and backs up into the conduit buffer, maintaining a blocked state, the converger accepts only the higher-priority <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span>. <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> cannot enter, so its recirculation is blocked. With that return path blocked, all 6/min of <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> enter the activation port, and the machine consumes <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> at 60/min. If the incoming supply is below 60/min, the amount of <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> in the setup gradually falls until it no longer occupies all the converger&#39;s input opportunities. <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> can then recirculate normally, reducing its activation flow to 3/min. The machine now consumes <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> at 30/min. If the incoming supply is above 30/min, <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> accumulates again until it occupies the converger and blocks <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> recirculation once more. This repeats until, <u>over a sufficiently long period, the amount of </u><span class="mark-red"><u>Cuprium Solution</u></span><u> buffered in the setup neither increases nor decreases</u>. The same approach can of course be used to solidify Heavy Xiranite, Hetonite, or Pyrrolite: only the input and activation materials change. <u>This is “feedback control” for a setup whose input material and activation material are different.</u> <span class="mark-grey">Or, when the incoming material supply is below 30/min: input overflows → the machine speeds up → input stops overflowing → the machine slows down → input overflows again, and so on.</span> [TN2]</p><p>Since the buffered <span class="mark-red">Cuprium Solution</span> neither increases nor decreases over a sufficiently long period, its incoming supply rate equals its consumption rate. <u>The input supply rate therefore controls the machine&#39;s operating rate: “feedforward.”</u></p><details><summary>A summary diagram for 言 — kokobird</summary><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6cb259b335e2794b.q90-efb40c42f28b1f4e.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p></details><details><summary>A video explanation for 言 — kokobird</summary><p>[Endfield / Version 1.5 Factory] Modular Blueprints and Technical Walkthrough: 120 Xiranite + 150 Cuprium = 18.4 Liquid Xiranite + Zero-Waste Hetonite and Heavy Xiranite. Jump to 07:09: <a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Jbt66XECE/?share_source=copy_web&amp;vd_source=ff7e542929960451adc95a08b74266a8&amp;t=429" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1Jbt66XECE/?share_source=copy_web&amp;vd_source=ff7e542929960451adc95a08b74266a8&amp;t=429</a></p></details><p>The following sections extend this setup. You can skip them on a first read, although taking a look would be even better.</p><p><span id="source-block-WWzr4gcf9ltyV0vuFEpITK"></span></p><h3 id="a-small-modification-lowering-the-minimum-safe-input-from-30-min-to-20-min" tabindex="-1">A Small Modification: Lowering the Minimum Safe Input from 30/min to 20/min <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-small-modification-lowering-the-minimum-safe-input-from-30-min-to-20-min" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A Small Modification: Lowering the Minimum Safe Input from 30/min to 20/min&quot;">​</a></h3><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-18fbd62ae95e8119.q90-413cea65509c4e6f.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>As shown above, route two of the splitter&#39;s outputs—the highlighted ones—back into the loop. When recirculation is unblocked, Liquid Xiranite enters the activation port at 6/3/min. The setup can then run without wasting Liquid Xiranite whenever the Cuprium Solution input exceeds 20/min.</p><p><span id="source-block-yspmniCYNV6aoreECUDXoH"></span></p><h3 id="reusing-a-splitter-to-save-pipe-components" tabindex="-1">Reusing a Splitter to Save Pipe Components <a class="header-anchor" href="#reusing-a-splitter-to-save-pipe-components" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reusing a Splitter to Save Pipe Components&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Splitting Liquid Xiranite requires a splitter, and creating the priority arrangement also requires a splitter. Why not use the same one for both? The following arrangement does exactly that; the shared splitter is highlighted.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-c5da20802ab18b58.q90-83d976fcaf6fa85c.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p><span class="mark-grey"><em>However, the splitter used for </em></span><span class="mark-green"><em>Liquid Xiranite</em></span><span class="mark-grey"><em> sits directly against the converger, with no buffer between them. If too much input material passes through the shared pipe, it can block Liquid Xiranite recirculation more than intended. Under certain input patterns, the machine can fail to build up a material reserve and waste activation material while starved of inputs, even though the supply is within the nominally safe 50–100% range. In most cases, though, this does not happen.</em></span></p><p><span id="source-block-GPVuKVjXpjJwV8QY13uNbW"></span></p><h3 id="using-overflow-to-remove-the-minimum-safe-input" tabindex="-1">Using Overflow to Remove the Minimum Safe Input <a class="header-anchor" href="#using-overflow-to-remove-the-minimum-safe-input" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Using Overflow to Remove the Minimum Safe Input&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An overflow arrangement reduces the minimum safe input to 0. The benefit is clear; the drawbacks are more components and a larger footprint.</p><p><span id="source-block-ld1GQm5eap1zGMtEv5FfUf"></span></p><h3 id="simplifying-the-overflow-arrangement-with-a-liquid-xiranite-bus" tabindex="-1">Simplifying the Overflow Arrangement with a Liquid Xiranite Bus <a class="header-anchor" href="#simplifying-the-overflow-arrangement-with-a-liquid-xiranite-bus" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Simplifying the Overflow Arrangement with a Liquid Xiranite Bus&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This removes the priority-merging section of the overflow arrangement. Instead, the bus services its upstream inputs in round-robin order, providing a form of merge priority and reducing both pipe-component count and footprint.</p><p><span id="source-block-0XgKNhUGDp6ohb2XKS7ItQ"></span></p><h3 id="risks-from-oscillating-input" tabindex="-1">Risks from Oscillating Input <a class="header-anchor" href="#risks-from-oscillating-input" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Risks from Oscillating Input&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Suppose the supply alternates between 10/min for x seconds and 60/min for x seconds. The average is 35/min, within the safe range. But if x is large enough for the machine to exhaust all the material in the setup during the x seconds at 10/min, it will waste Liquid Xiranite while running without enough inputs. If the upstream supply oscillates, watch out for this. Add buffer capacity or regularize/filter the incoming flow if necessary. A simple flow regularizer is—or will be—shown below:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-6008cd20ad06a7a1.q90-b6a70f85caad1e46.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><div class="logistics-image-caption" data-source-block="bqnQwrYwA0VV3L5RwHAnNp"><p>When idle, the acid can escape from the loop. Add a Pipe Control Port or make sure there is another fluid inside.</p></div><p>kokobird: A four-unit <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> setup is a spectacular example. Whenever <span class="mark-red">Hetonite Gas</span> fills up, there is a delay between <span class="mark-yellow">Acridgen</span> overflowing and <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Gas</span> production starting. Eight or nine units of <span class="mark-yellow">Acridgen</span> always spill over. But a single unit of <span class="mark-yellow">Acridgen</span> already keeps all four <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> units running for 10 seconds. Running continuously for over a minute consumes a frightening amount of material—even a single-port conduit supplying <span class="mark-red">Hetonite Gas</span> may not keep up. The <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Gas</span> output during that period is also enormous, potentially exceeding the downstream solidification rate. That causes the <span class="mark-green">Xiragen</span> used for solidification to keep overflowing. Even after <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Gas</span> production stops, this accumulated <span class="mark-green">Xiragen</span> can still drive the consumption of several hundred units of <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Gas</span>. That&#39;s why you need a Gas Tank before the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Gas</span> solidification stage.</p><p><span id="source-block-GpAMDqu6wRWBWak2xiqhpC"></span></p><h3 id="isolating-the-control-circuit-and-replacing-mixed-fluid-pipes-with-an-unpowered-reactor-crucible" tabindex="-1">Isolating the Control Circuit and Replacing Mixed-Fluid Pipes with an Unpowered Reactor Crucible <a class="header-anchor" href="#isolating-the-control-circuit-and-replacing-mixed-fluid-pipes-with-an-unpowered-reactor-crucible" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Isolating the Control Circuit and Replacing Mixed-Fluid Pipes with an Unpowered Reactor Crucible&quot;">​</a></h3><p><span id="source-block-vOUgIKsx6J4Hs9rAMRIMIM"></span></p><h2 id="an-overview-of-ratio-control-across-different-fluids" tabindex="-1">An Overview of Ratio Control Across Different Fluids <a class="header-anchor" href="#an-overview-of-ratio-control-across-different-fluids" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;An Overview of Ratio Control Across Different Fluids&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This section was mainly written by kokobird.</p><p><span class="mark-grey"># 言: This is background for filling-and-separating control, though it has other uses too. If you only want to learn Fluid-Gas control, you can skip it for now—but flow copying will still come up, so you will have to return eventually (?)</span></p><p>For a single fluid, you can use a splitter–converger arrangement directly: for example, diverting 1/5 of the Liquid Xiranite to activate a Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit, or diverting 1/11 of the Xiragen to solidify purified Heavy Xiranite. [TN3] But when the input material and activation material are different fluids, establishing a relationship such as “1 unit of B for every 10 units of A” requires another technique.</p><p>In general, the flow being measured must be returned to its original production line after measurement, so most of these approaches copy the signal. Think of it as making an impression: start with a template, let X items of A leave X empty spaces in it, then use those spaces to receive X items of B. Our main task is to build that template using the game&#39;s mechanics.</p><p><span id="source-block-ZRO4iuJDMSiB0HnEkMTg51"></span></p><h3 id="feedforward-using-filling-and-separating-units-—-both-fluids-must-be-in-the-same-phase" tabindex="-1">Feedforward Using Filling and Separating Units — Both Fluids Must Be in the Same Phase <a class="header-anchor" href="#feedforward-using-filling-and-separating-units-—-both-fluids-must-be-in-the-same-phase" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Feedforward Using Filling and Separating Units — Both Fluids Must Be in the Same Phase&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Both must be liquids, or both must be gases.</p><p>The following setup controls the ratio of <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> to <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Gas</span> at 1:10. [TN4] <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span> exits the conduit outlet at the upper right. Half goes directly into the transmuter; the other half enters a Filling Unit to be measured. The Filling Unit has plenty of empty bottles, so every <span class="mark-rose_red">unit of Cuprium Solution</span> entering it produces one bottle of <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span>. That <span class="mark-rose_red">bottled solution</span> immediately enters the next Separating Unit, which extracts the liquid and returns it to the Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit.</p><p>From that unit of <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span>&#39;s perspective, all it has done is take a detour. So what has changed? The Separating Unit has released an empty bottle! It has five connected outputs: four return bottles directly to the stash, while the fifth sends bottles into a Filling Unit full of <span class="mark-green">LIQUID! XIRANITE!</span> A bottle takes one unit of <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> and goes into another Separating Unit. Think back through the chain: each bottle taking this route means five bottles have left the first Separating Unit, which means five units of <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span> entered the first Filling Unit, which means ten units of <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span> left the conduit outlet in total. Thus, for every 10 units of <span class="mark-rose_red">Cuprium Solution</span>, exactly 1 unit of <span class="mark-green">Liquid Xiranite</span> enters the Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit, supplying precisely what is needed.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-f31fa73eee44395e.q90-649c02c1e781149b.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>For more examples, see <a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1yp3q6GEK3/?share_source=copy_web&amp;vd_source=3b974227efd90987cb6375fbe49d61b8&amp;t=393" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">my version 1.4 automatic-balancing plan video</a>. These mainly include Cuprium Solution feedforward control, which measures Cuprium Solution; Pyrrolite solidification feedforward control, which measures Hetonite Gas; and an Acridgen feedback-control setup, which also measures Hetonite Gas.</p><p>Because Filling and Separating Units consume quite a lot of power and take up considerable space, most later designs used the other mechanisms on this page. Still, I think filling-and-separating ratio control may be the easiest approach for beginners to understand. <s>It also uses relatively few pipe components, comes with a built-in five-way splitter, does not depend on priority, runs as precisely as meshing gears, responds quickly, and has small oscillations.<br> 言: Really? As someone who had only just come back in 1.4, I practically fainted when I saw this solid-item flow-copying contraption.</s></p><p><span id="source-block-7wWfjywD6y5qN3j05ZbtZr"></span></p><h3 id="output-driven-control-using-filling-and-separating-units-—-the-fluids-may-be-in-different-phases" tabindex="-1">Output-Driven Control Using Filling and Separating Units — The Fluids May Be in Different Phases <a class="header-anchor" href="#output-driven-control-using-filling-and-separating-units-—-the-fluids-may-be-in-different-phases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Output-Driven Control Using Filling and Separating Units — The Fluids May Be in Different Phases&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This is also explained in the video above. Developed by 智乃, the setup mixes canisters and bottles on a belt at a 5:1 ratio. Each unit of Acridgen passing through fills a canister and advances the mixed belt by one space. After five spaces, a bottle moves on to receive 1 unit of Liquid Xiranite, enabling the production of 5 Acridgen.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-d47bce58da88599f.q90-09aaab70a6e328bd.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p><span id="source-block-UxB6wgFGoOINnRzbFQRMRY"></span></p><h3 id="using-empty-slots-in-a-fluid-loop" tabindex="-1">Using Empty Slots in a Fluid Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#using-empty-slots-in-a-fluid-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Using Empty Slots in a Fluid Loop&quot;">​</a></h3><p>I discussed this with @花底月莺 on Bilibili, and this approach works too. <s>It was actually the first design I wanted to try, but the counts came out wrong in testing, so I abandoned it. Looking back, that was probably the “4n, 1 s stall” issue.</s></p><p>This is essentially a fluid version of the solid-item tape method. Use a third liquid—water in the diagram—as the circulating medium. Downstream of the Fluid Tank, first merge it with the fluid being measured—Liquid Xiranite in the diagram—and separate them again. Farther downstream, merge it with the fluid to be released—Sewage in the diagram—and separate them again. Every empty slot that Liquid Xiranite creates by blocking the water can then accommodate a unit of Sewage. The red arrows show the priority directions.</p><p>Be aware that this setup is prone to the “4n, 1 s stall” phenomenon (<a href="/endfield-skydoc/introduction-to-analyzing-endfield-logistics-bugs">Introduction to Analyzing Endfield Logistics Bugs</a>). Before putting it into service, check that the fluid loop runs at 120. The two flow meters at the bottom of the diagram are for testing; in actual use, a single pipe connection is enough. [TN5]</p><p>@花底月莺 used this approach to build an adaptive Heavy Xiranite line with three production units. Depending on the gas supplied from the vents, it adjusts Liquid Xiranite consumption to support Heavy Xiranite production in the Solid-Gas Transmuting Units, and stops when Heavy Xiranite storage fills up. It accepts up to 150 of that gas supply to reach the output rate of three Heavy Xiranite units. <a href="https://www.xiaoheihe.cn/app/bbs/link/74ee7297d34d" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Xiaoheihe link</a> [TN5]</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-63b0f1ebe5aac74d.q90-d417f1877418ee4f.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p><span id="source-block-KQMwH9m91p61ZbV7SIdCQF"></span></p><h2 id="heavy-xiranite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Heavy Xiranite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#heavy-xiranite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Heavy Xiranite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two approaches:</p><p>Maintain a 5:1 ratio between Heavy Xiragen and Xiragen, mainly through flow copying (see <a href="/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups#source-block-vOUgIKsx6J4Hs9rAMRIMIM">Some Simpler Adaptive Setups</a>). Early filling-and-separating designs converted this into solid-item flow copying because liquid/gas flow-copying techniques were not yet mature.</p><p>Directly splitting Xiragen at 10:1 is a useful shortcut.</p><p><span id="source-block-QT0CJjZ3t6r0Vuy48ELcl6"></span></p><h2 id="heavy-xiranite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Heavy Xiranite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#heavy-xiranite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Heavy Xiranite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-4d8a6b88cbfa7753.q90-dc33d0fa7ee2b41b.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>Production recipe: 1 Xiragen (activation) + 10 Xiragen (purified into 10 Heavy Xiragen) = 2 Heavy Xiranite. <span class="mark-grey">#Sufficient Inergen environment / Separator Cores</span></p><p>Feedforward: the input supply rate determines the machine&#39;s operating rate.</p><p>Feedback control: the machine runs at a high rate when inputs overflow and at a low rate when they do not.</p><p>Treat one Purification Unit–Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit group as “one Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit.” The principle is then exactly the same as in <a href="/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups#source-block-pn4Iehl7uCFHMbdr1hj0r1">this section</a>.</p><p>Thus, <u>whenever activation material enters this “Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit,” sufficient input material is available.</u> Provided the output is not blocked (all the Xiragen produced is consumed downstream) and activation input does not exceed 6/min (easily achieved with a Control Port set to 6/min), no activation material will be wasted. [TN9]</p><p>For input supply from 0/min–36/min: input overflows → speed up → input stops overflowing → slow down → input overflows again. This repeated cycle <u>demonstrates feedback control.</u></p><p>The output rate clearly depends on the input rate: <u>this demonstrates feedforward.</u></p><p>The <strong>phase problem</strong> also applies here. The Conduit Outlet Manifold shown must connect to a single-port Conduit Inlet; see <a href="/endfield-skydoc/some-simpler-adaptive-setups">Some Simpler Adaptive Setups</a>.</p><p><span id="source-block-ckClPl3A1wZT5aMlXRFkvh"></span></p><h3 id="treating-two-purification–solid-gas-groups-as-one-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit" tabindex="-1">Treating Two Purification–Solid-Gas Groups as “One Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit” <a class="header-anchor" href="#treating-two-purification–solid-gas-groups-as-one-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Treating Two Purification–Solid-Gas Groups as “One Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit”&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If one Purification–Solid-Gas group can be treated as one Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit, what about two? That works too.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-814afa5d6120e1f9.q90-155173a599b14e17.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><p>The production recipe can then be regarded as:</p><p>2 Xiragen (activation) + 20 Xiragen (purified into 10 Heavy Xiragen) = 4 Heavy Xiranite. <span class="mark-grey">#Sufficient Inergen environment / Separator Cores</span> [TN9]</p><p>Do not forget the <strong>phase problem</strong>. Unlike before, a single-port conduit can carry at most 120/min, so the manifold outlet in the image receives at most 120/min Xiragen. One Purification–Solid-Gas group can process up to 66/min Xiragen into Heavy Xiranite (66/min=60/min for Heavy Xiragen production +6/min for Solid-Gas activation). Two groups should therefore accept 132/min. If only 120/min can enter, would sufficient Xiragen still leave some machine capacity unused?</p><p>The researchers are thinking deeply...</p><p><span id="source-block-Wi0XahCYOfQzZbiJEKVEQI"></span></p><h3 id="treating-a-larger-structure-as-one-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit" tabindex="-1">Treating a Larger Structure as “One Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit” <a class="header-anchor" href="#treating-a-larger-structure-as-one-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Treating a Larger Structure as “One Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit”&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If the starting point is Liquid Xiranite rather than Xiragen,</p><p><span id="source-block-ZCNK3cAsSj0evFP4vunpsX"></span></p><h2 id="pyrrolite-multiple-solid-gas-transmuting-units-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Pyrrolite: Multiple Solid-Gas Transmuting Units — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#pyrrolite-multiple-solid-gas-transmuting-units-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pyrrolite: Multiple Solid-Gas Transmuting Units — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Treat several machines as a single system. For example, regard three Solid-Gas Transmuting Units as one machine consuming up to 90/min of Pyrrolite Gas as input and 18/min of Xiragen for activation, then connect a feedback-control module. [TN6]</p><p>Make sure input material accumulates during low-rate operation. Consumption by units not directly connected to the control module must be less than or equal to consumption by those directly connected. When Xiragen is split equally among the three activation ports, this means the input supplied to the units not directly connected must be greater than or equal to the input supplied to the directly connected units. You do not have to split activation material equally, provided the consumption condition above is met; in this three-unit case, however, an unequal split would not save any more pipe components. Also avoid raising the actual minimum safe input through excessive material flow in a design that reuses a splitter. The directly connected unit count is given as 2/2, 1, or 1/2; anything lower serves no purpose. Here, the count is 1. [TN6]</p><p><span id="source-block-nGCCd7T8zJ0hIFpUm3u04B"></span></p><h2 id="pyrrolite-gas-multiple-gas-reactor-globes-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Pyrrolite Gas: Multiple Gas Reactor Globes — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#pyrrolite-gas-multiple-gas-reactor-globes-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pyrrolite Gas: Multiple Gas Reactor Globes — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With sufficient Xiragen available, treat three Gas Reactor Globes as one machine consuming up to 180/min of Hetonite Gas as input and 6/min of Acridgen for activation.</p><p>Make sure input material accumulates during low-rate operation. Consumption by units not directly connected to the control module must be less than or equal to consumption by the directly connected units. Since the Acridgen Gas Dispersing Unit controls all three Gas Reactor Globes in exactly the same way, this directly translates to the input supplied to the units not directly connected being greater than or equal to the input supplied to the directly connected units. Again, avoid raising the actual minimum safe input through excessive material flow in a design that reuses a splitter. The directly connected unit count is given as 2/2, 1, or 1/2; anything lower serves no purpose. Here, the count is 1. [TN6]</p><p><span id="source-block-dRiqji5Pa4BrTG607tnesV"></span></p><h2 id="acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Keep Acridgen adequately supplied without wasting Liquid Xiranite. This may be the only part of a gross-output setup that genuinely requires output-driven control. (Actually, it isn&#39;t: a clever workaround comes later.)</p><p>Give Acridgen priority. When excess Acridgen overflows, it blocks the Liquid Xiranite used for activation from reaching the activation port.</p><p><span id="source-block-HUDwTTEVX7SMgbBJJ5qB31"></span></p><h3 id="acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Before Liquid Xiranite enters the input-side feedback module, route it through the output-side feedback module. This implements AND logic: Liquid Xiranite may enter the activation port only when sufficient input material is available <strong>and</strong> the output is not blocked.</p><p>The input-side module can, of course, use an overflow arrangement that adapts across the full 100% range.</p><p>Yes, this is entirely unnecessary for Acridgen. What this section actually presents is a general AND-combined input- and output-driven control scheme for cases where the input, product, and activation material are all different.</p><p><span id="source-block-927DmCsBL8fp8fjP6fISM4"></span></p><h2 id="cuprium-gas-multiple-fluid-gas-transmuting-units-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Cuprium Gas: Multiple Fluid-Gas Transmuting Units — Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#cuprium-gas-multiple-fluid-gas-transmuting-units-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cuprium Gas: Multiple Fluid-Gas Transmuting Units — Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If feedforward can control multiple units, so can output-driven control. Treat the group as one machine.</p><p><span id="source-block-zmBKtZ8iUEhQ3VyLjCLk3M"></span></p><h3 id="cuprium-gas-multiple-fluid-gas-transmuting-units-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Cuprium Gas: Multiple Fluid-Gas Transmuting Units — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#cuprium-gas-multiple-fluid-gas-transmuting-units-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cuprium Gas: Multiple Fluid-Gas Transmuting Units — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Multiple units can also use AND logic between input- and output-driven control. Treat them as one machine and connect the combined control module.</p><p><span id="source-block-MWuvhZkwIaZ3JRFUIkZKwO"></span></p><h3 id="isolated-control-centralized-control-equal-split-control" tabindex="-1">Isolated Control? Centralized Control! Equal-Split Control?! <a class="header-anchor" href="#isolated-control-centralized-control-equal-split-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Isolated Control? Centralized Control! Equal-Split Control?!&quot;">​</a></h3><p>With the following arrangement, the feedback cycle moves from the machine alone—“input overflows → speed up → input stops overflowing → slow down → input overflows again”—to the Conduit Outlet Manifold and the machine together. The supported flow can clearly reach 240/min, allowing more units to be connected. Isolating the control circuit therefore lends itself naturally to centralized control of multiple units.</p><p>But is 240 the upper limit? Of course not! Consider the feedback arrangements for multiple Solid-Gas and Fluid-Gas Transmuting Units. To keep flow low enough to reuse a splitter, only the supply to some of the machines passes through the control module. Likewise, we can draw, say, 30 from each of several 240 streams, merge those portions, and pass them through an isolated control module. This lets us control all the modules through equal splitting. In practice, one control module can serve enough units to consume up to 120/min of activation material. More than 120/min is possible too, but that level of demand seems unlikely in the foreseeable future: 1200 Cuprium Solution input, plus the obvious demand for solid Cuprium, would far exceed even the Ferrium Ore supply in the valley.</p><p><span id="source-block-no8mFWFgz8MM6gUqsnmvdE"></span></p><h2 id="acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#acridgen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Acridgen: Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use blocking to maintain a 5:1 ratio between product and activation material. An elegant design, but it cannot easily be converted to isolated control, let alone centralized control.</p><p><span id="source-block-X55F4HMCEWgz89R3mKCyU8"></span></p><h2 id="acridgen-sharing-a-full-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Acridgen: Sharing a Full-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#acridgen-sharing-a-full-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Acridgen: Sharing a Full-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Notice that Acridgen output and acid input are in a 1:1 ratio. Why not use Acridgen consumption to control the acid supply? So simple.</p><p><span id="source-block-DGOeQqsHhmKgbM8QjjJGki"></span></p><h3 id="sharing-a-full-rate-cuprium-gas-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Sharing a Full-Rate Cuprium Gas Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#sharing-a-full-rate-cuprium-gas-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sharing a Full-Rate Cuprium Gas Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Sharing with Xiragen production and sharing with Cuprium Gas production are much the same. The main difference is that Cuprium Solution reacts two units at a time. An extra input is needed to prevent the machine from being left with just one unit of Cuprium Solution, then going to sleep.</p><p><span id="source-block-AiiZV7HBrHkNnZ4FrUFOaG"></span></p><h3 id="sharing-a-part-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" tabindex="-1">Sharing a Part-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#sharing-a-part-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-ratio-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sharing a Part-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Ratio Control&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Does it have to run at full rate? Whether the machine is producing Acridgen or Xiragen, its activity is reflected in the Liquid Xiranite overflow, since the Acridgen demand is small. If Acridgen demand were much larger, this would become the adaptive two-input arrangement described below for combined Pyrrolite and Heavy Xiranite production in a Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit. Even so, it may be worth using that arrangement to avoid oscillation in certain production plans, such as maximum net output. Let the overflowing Liquid Xiranite serve as activation material, and the shared machine can run below full rate as well.</p><p><span id="source-block-If0ndoaEGze9w3F70BvQBw"></span></p><h3 id="sharing-a-full-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-a-feedforward-exception" tabindex="-1">Sharing a Full-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — A Feedforward Exception <a class="header-anchor" href="#sharing-a-full-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-a-feedforward-exception" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sharing a Full-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — A Feedforward Exception&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Even 1:1 control uses too many pipe components—a whole two! What if the three Gas Reactor Globes directly controlled the acid supply instead? The acid could enter the shared machine, become Acridgen, and then feed the three reactors. This completely eliminates the need for output-driven Acridgen control. The drawback is a somewhat slower feedback response from the reactors.</p><p><span id="source-block-mBB5twcVdkPuz0o2apJEZz"></span></p><h3 id="sharing-a-part-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-a-feedforward-exception" tabindex="-1">Sharing a Part-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — A Feedforward Exception <a class="header-anchor" href="#sharing-a-part-rate-xiragen-fluid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-a-feedforward-exception" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sharing a Part-Rate Xiragen Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit — A Feedforward Exception&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Does it have to run at full rate? Same idea as above.</p><p><span id="source-block-8Q1jGfLgKGQRMQyzcj5kZy"></span></p><h2 id="heavy-xiranite-sharing-a-full-rate-pyrrolite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Heavy Xiranite: Sharing a Full-Rate Pyrrolite Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#heavy-xiranite-sharing-a-full-rate-pyrrolite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Heavy Xiranite: Sharing a Full-Rate Pyrrolite Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Excess Pyrrolite Gas. Heavy Xiranite: 25 at most. [TN5]</p><p><span id="source-block-k1drcAjTKyWtJyNkZTpUDk"></span></p><h3 id="adaptive-control-for-two-input-materials" tabindex="-1">Adaptive Control for Two Input Materials <a class="header-anchor" href="#adaptive-control-for-two-input-materials" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adaptive Control for Two Input Materials&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Two-input adaptive control simply lets both input materials control the Xiragen supply through feedback.</p><p><span id="source-block-T6EKZtC2vBfxuJjpApsqOB"></span></p><h2 id="cuprium-gas-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Cuprium Gas: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#cuprium-gas-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cuprium Gas: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use an unpowered Reactor Crucible to propagate blocking from a solid material to a liquid.</p><p><span id="source-block-DIFAJeoG5AObFYAxgGb3Zc"></span></p><h2 id="pyrrolite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Pyrrolite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#pyrrolite-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pyrrolite: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use an unpowered Reactor Crucible to propagate blocking from a solid material to a liquid.</p><p>Essentially, this changes the input composition of the multi-unit Pyrrolite Solid-Gas Transmuting setup and uses the arrangement in <a href="/endfield-skydoc/research-on-theory-and-practice-of-complex-serial-parallel-mixed">Theory and Practice of Complex Serial/Parallel Mixed Production</a>.</p><p><span id="source-block-ITQ8P733EvTairWtUV4EBF"></span></p><h3 id="pyrrolite-hetonite-and-xiranite-—-a-part-rate-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-with-three-inputs" tabindex="-1">Pyrrolite, Hetonite, and Xiranite — A Part-Rate Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit with Three Inputs <a class="header-anchor" href="#pyrrolite-hetonite-and-xiranite-—-a-part-rate-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-with-three-inputs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pyrrolite, Hetonite, and Xiranite — A Part-Rate Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit with Three Inputs&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Adding Xiragen from the North Wuling Exclusion Zone as an input does not make things complicated, because Xiragen can be split directly at 5:1. [TN7]</p><p><span id="source-block-R0yRbvDqSuO40bHFNIlyWX"></span></p><h2 id="heavy-xiragen-and-hetonite-gas-a-shared-purification-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" tabindex="-1">Heavy Xiragen and Hetonite Gas: A Shared Purification Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control <a class="header-anchor" href="#heavy-xiragen-and-hetonite-gas-a-shared-purification-unit-—-feedforward-with-feedback-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Heavy Xiragen and Hetonite Gas: A Shared Purification Unit — Feedforward with Feedback Control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I haven&#39;t fully worked out the details yet. Setting this aside for now.</p><p><span id="source-block-ZJVOO6eIEMkLMbQvKmSE1N"></span></p><h2 id="pyrrolite-and-hetonite-a-shared-part-rate-solid-gas-transmuting-setup-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" tabindex="-1">Pyrrolite and Hetonite: A Shared Part-Rate Solid-Gas Transmuting Setup — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#pyrrolite-and-hetonite-a-shared-part-rate-solid-gas-transmuting-setup-—-and-combined-input-and-output-driven-control-with-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pyrrolite and Hetonite: A Shared Part-Rate Solid-Gas Transmuting Setup — AND-Combined Input- and Output-Driven Control with Feedback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A very cool adaptive production line.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-4e5e84b5d041b1d0.q90-ed441470533d56e5.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><div class="logistics-image-caption" data-source-block="BRAbUyENxN6mouDw0bWYhW"><p>EF011I9oIIea9UO030O7</p></div><p><span class="mark-red">This blueprint has problems and is outdated.</span></p><p>When neither Hetonite nor Pyrrolite storage is full, produce Hetonite and Pyrrolite at a 1:2 ratio.</p><p>When Hetonite storage is full but Pyrrolite storage is not, all three units produce Pyrrolite.</p><p>When Hetonite storage is not full but Pyrrolite storage is, one unit produces Hetonite.</p><p>When both are full, of course the setup goes on strike—the unused inputs accumulate. What more do you want?</p><p>If you use some Hetonite after both products have filled up, the setup does not reset automatically. You have to sell some Pyrrolite to reset it. In fact, selling Pyrrolite is such a natural part of operating the line that we did not include a Pyrrolite-full detector; the setup relies on selling it to reset.</p><p><span id="source-block-MPkNbUiCtrQPRpxKeiP4o5"></span></p><h2 id="xiragen-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-using-overflow" tabindex="-1">Xiragen: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control Using Overflow <a class="header-anchor" href="#xiragen-solid-gas-transmuting-unit-—-output-driven-control-using-overflow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Xiragen: Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit — Output-Driven Control Using Overflow&quot;">​</a></h2><p>kokobird: Boss, here&#39;s a contribution.</p><p>未来科技&#39;s requirement: the upstream line supplies 18 Xiragen, while downstream demand varies between 18 and 48. The Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit needs to start and stop on demand. [TN5]</p><p>How it works: the external gas supply first enters a recirculation loop. One branch shares a pipe with any other fluid; the mixed-fluid section must be more than 1 grid cell long. When the other fluid is flowing, it blocks the shared pipe. Xiragen first accumulates in the Gas Tank, then overflows into the Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit, which starts operating normally. Total output now reaches 48. Because downstream demand is below 48, material gradually backs up until it blocks the other fluid. The Xiragen on the left can then take both routes, so the overflow stops. Eventually, the Solid-Gas Transmuting Unit stops as well, returning output to 18. Output is now insufficient for downstream demand, so the other fluid eventually starts flowing again. This creates an adaptive oscillation cycle.</p><p>The drawbacks are the usual ones for overflow designs: slow startup, slow response, and long cycles. Let the upstream flow be I and downstream demand be O’. The actual total demand is O, given by <code>O&#39;+(O&#39;-I)*1.25</code>. Let downstream storage capacity be C. Startup takes approximately <code>500/(I/2)</code>, after which Xiranite production begins. A further <code>C/(30+I-O)</code> elapses before the other fluid is blocked. If this takes too long, the Conduit Outlet Manifold fills with C0 Xiragen. Recovery after blocking then takes <code>C0*10(~5000s)</code>, and the extra Xiranite production driven by this Xiragen reaches <code>C0*10(~5000s)*(30+I-O&#39;)</code>. A fairly large buffer is needed. [TN8]</p><p><a href="https://beta.enka.network/endfield/aic/?id=W2c5B3w8Z4U3t5" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://beta.enka.network/endfield/aic/?id=W2c5B3w8Z4U3t5</a></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-f9efe8b718b26492.q90-009c4a7c970a51ce.webp" alt="Some Simpler Adaptive Setups"></p><hr><p><strong>Translator&#39;s notes</strong></p><p>These notes identify ambiguities in the source. They are not corrections to the design or independent verification of its mechanics.</p><blockquote><p><strong>TN1 — Activation timing and the 6/min limit.</strong> The opening distinguishes average supply from the interval between activation events, but later states that a Control Port set to 6/min is sufficient. That assertion is retained as the author&#39;s claim; a mean rate alone does not establish event spacing. In the Cuprium Gas section, the source also says “below 6/min” while recommending a limit of exactly 6/min. The strict inequality is retained. <strong>TN2 — Input ranges.</strong> The current first Xiragen section uses 0/min–36/min. The Cuprium Gas section develops a 30/min–60/min safe range, but its grey summary says “below 30/min.” Both ranges are translated as written rather than silently reconciled. <strong>TN3 — The single-fluid split.</strong> The overview says to divert 1/5 of Liquid Xiranite for activation, whereas the earlier recipe and ratio-control example specify 5 units for production plus 1 for activation. The intended denominator is not explained there; 1/5 is retained. <strong>TN4 — Cuprium Gas versus Cuprium Solution.</strong> The filling-and-separating section initially states a 1:10 ratio between Liquid Xiranite and Cuprium Gas. Its subsequent explanation measures Cuprium Solution and derives 1 Liquid Xiranite per 10 Cuprium Solution. The initial material name is retained and flagged. <strong>TN5 — Shorthand and omitted units.</strong> The source leaves units implicit for several values, including the fluid-loop speed of 120, the gas input of 150, the Heavy Xiranite maximum of 25, and the final setup&#39;s supply of 18 and demand of 18–48. This translation does not silently add units. The source shorthand refers to gas vents; here it denotes their gas supply, not the vents themselves as input material. <strong>TN6 — Multi-unit notes.</strong> The current multi-unit Solid-Gas section is headed “Pyrrolite.” Both multi-unit feedback sections give a “directly connected unit count” of <code>2/2</code>, <code>1</code>, or <code>1/2</code> without explaining the notation. These have been retained rather than treated as an ordinary count of whole machines or rewritten into a guessed ratio. <strong>TN9 — New Heavy Xiranite examples.</strong> The one-group paragraph calls its downstream output Xiragen even though the recipe produces Heavy Xiranite; that name is retained. The two-group recipe says 20 Xiragen are purified into 10 Heavy Xiragen, whereas the one-group example says 10 into 10. These source statements and the 120/min, 132/min and 0/min–36/min figures are preserved without repairing the proposed design. <strong>TN7 — Unclear wording.</strong> “Xiragen from the North Wuling Exclusion Zone” uses the confirmed regional shorthand. The preceding heading lists solid Xiranite, whereas the sentence names Xiragen; this has not been harmonized. <strong>TN8 — Final paragraph.</strong> The final section discusses Xiragen output but switches to “Xiranite production” in its timing discussion. The material names and formulas are preserved as written. The formulas mix flow quantities, time expressions, and <code>~5000s</code> without fully stating their units, so no dimensional correction has been imposed.</p></blockquote><p>Obvious naming slips such as 息壤液/息壤溶液 for 液化息壤 and 液气转化剂 for 液气转化机 have been rendered using the established names Liquid Xiranite and Fluid-Gas Transmuting Unit. Unfinished headings and outline fragments remain unfinished, as in the source.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
